## Decision and scope

Replace subject-only merging with cause-based triage. This new record proposes to supersede
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/); that historical decision stays intact.
The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) says to merge by subject, but its interactive examples already
compare causes. Its old review date and stale warning are reasons to verify the rule against the live components.

The [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471/) says one issue and one queued fix.
Its rendered cause table contradicts that prose: the merged reports cover a shared-parser problem
and a separate booking-form hint problem. Neither component proves that a product fix has shipped.

## What the export shows

<InvestigationSummary />

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Counts come from the exported records, deduplicated by report ID and filtered to this ticket,
then joined to the tenant directory. Browser locale and intake tags are clues, not the grouping rule.
The `dayFirst` function asks the platform's locale formatter for date-part order.

## Separate fixes

**Shared parser:** day-first tenants send dates through a parser that reads month first.
For example, Harbour Dental's report R-41200 describes 7 April becoming 4 July.
Make parsing agree with the tenant's date order and check app, email, and booking-v2 paths.
The app and email paths for booking-v3 tenants still belong to this parser group.

**Form hint:** booking-v3 shows `MM/DD/YYYY` while its form interprets entered dates using the tenant's day-first order.
The cause applies to the form channel for Alder Physio, Copper Studio, and Pale Harbour.
Report R-41230 at Copper Studio is tagged parser by intake but belongs here.
Make the displayed hint and form interpretation agree. A parser-only fix does not correct a misleading hint.

These fixes are proposed from the KB's classification logic and reports. The exported KB does not contain
the product parser or form implementation, so product reproduction and regression checks remain engineering work.

## When the desk acted

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived before the large July merge. Read received and merged dates as different events.
This is the export through 4 October, not a claim about today's live queue or a resolved incident.

## Rule for the next report

Read the body, look up the tenant's locale and form, and identify the input channel.
Merge only when the evidence supports the same cause. Matching subjects can hide different causes;
different subjects can describe the same cause. If the tenant is unknown or the evidence conflicts,
keep the report separate for investigation instead of forcing a merge.

<DedupeExplorer />

## Follow-through

Keep a separate work ticket and report count for each cause. Retain the timing anomalies as evidence
and do not mark them fixed merely because the month/day problems are fixed.
Rebuild this page and recheck the report groups whenever the desk provides a newer export.
If counts, tenant configuration, or classifications change, update the corresponding tickets and record the change.

